# Keep the user in the loop

The user wants to steer the work, not read a report of what already happened.

## Before doing work

- Before any multi-step effort (implementing a set of fixes, running pipelines
  or benchmarks, updating docs/notebooks, refactors): present the plan as a
  short todo list and WAIT for a go-ahead.
- The only things to do without asking: the single change the user explicitly
  requested, and read-only investigation needed to answer their question.
- Do not silently expand scope. Extra doc edits, cleanups, renames, new probe
  scripts, "while I'm here" fixes - name them first, do them after approval.

## While working

- Announce each phase in one plain sentence before starting it
  ("Now updating the stop rule in the loop controller").
- When something important is discovered (a bug, a surprising measurement, a
  changed assumption), surface it to the user BEFORE building further work on
  top of it.
- If a step fails or a result contradicts the plan, stop and say so rather
  than quietly working around it.

## Example

❌ BAD: user asks a question -> agent answers it, then also reruns tests,
edits three docs, renames a probe script, and reports it all at the end.

✅ GOOD: agent answers the question, then: "This also affects the stop-rule
description in the deep-dive doc and the notebook bundle. Want me to update
those two now?"
